env file contains the 
supabase url: just the url ofthe supabase project
supabase service role kjey: legacy api key  private from the supabase project

together these two allows the backend to connect to the db 

jwt secret is used to sign jwt tokne,s they can be anything random 

pass is the defualt password used in the original seed scrit 

frontned url is the full url where the frontned is runnign along with the port. by default it is normally 5173 

database url is the full subapase conenction string, it is used to seed the db 

seeding:
there are 2 types of seeding 
1. npm run db:init : this drop severythign and seeds all the data in the orignal seed script, which is in ./test-data/init.sql . used to restore the app to the fresh state
2. npm run db:seed : this scripts clears all data and only installs the testing data 

togther these 2 will be used to setup the db to a testable state 
warning only do it in test db not in production 

to reset db to how it was before test, simply run db:init


we are using playwright instead of selenium


in postman, I have done 
const json = pm.response.json();
pm.collectionVariables.set("token", json.access_token);
pm.collectionVariables.set("student_user_id", json.user.id);

which saves the token to the memory, so in further requestrs we can use it. 
All other requests in the collection should set their Authorization header to:
Bearer {{token}}

I am using a seperate folder for tests, and some unit test cause module imports issues, 
the fix i found was to create a seperaete entry in moduleNameMapper in jest config
but for now I found a better one which tries to load all modules from backend, 
otherwise fall back to mapper. In case there are issues later on, try to 
manually map liek this 
// Direct Jest to use the backend's bcryptjs so that mocked values apply to both projects
        "^bcryptjs$": "<rootDir>/../iba-backend/node_modules/bcryptjs", 


        3. bookings.service.test.ts — cancel maps to "rejected" not "cancelled"
typescript// Your test expects:
status: "rejected", // Acts as cancelled
You noted this yourself with the comment. This is actually a real defect in the backend — cancel() calls updateStatus(id, 'rejected', ...) which sets status to "rejected" instead of "cancelled". Your test is correctly mirroring the buggy behaviour, but you should log this as a defect:
DEF-006 | TC-CANCEL-001 | S3 | cancel() sets status to "rejected" instead of "cancelled"